                <pb facs="0457" n="423" sameAs="frus:frus1958-60v09/pg423"/>

                <head>163. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>At 4:30 p.m. on May 15, Khrushchev, again accompanied by Gromyko, Vinogradov, and
                    Malinovsky, paid a visit to Macmillan and reiterated the demands he had made in
                    his conversation with De Gaulle during the morning (see <ref target="#d160"
                        >Document 160</ref>), but did not leave a copy of the declaration with the
                    Prime Minister. For McMillan’s account of this meeting, see <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Pointing the Way</hi>, pages 202–203. After Khrushchev
                    left, the British briefed both French and U.S. officials on the conversation. No
                    record of the British briefing has been found in Department of State files or at
                    the Eisenhower Library.</p>
            </div>
